## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests running-statistics in streaming data and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- Running median with two heaps; balance invariant; tie handling for even n.
- Complexity: O(log n) per insert, O(1) median; memory O(n).

## Code Example
```python
import heapq
class MedianMaint:
    def __init__(self):
        self.L = []  # max-heap via negatives
        self.R = []  # min-heap
    def add(self, x):
        if not self.L or x <= -self.L[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, x)
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R) + 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, -heapq.heappop(self.L))
        elif len(self.R) > len(self.L):
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -heapq.heappop(self.R))
    def median(self):
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R):
            return -self.L[0]
        return (-self.L[0] + self.R[0]) / 2
```

